{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Built and ran the probe for @q_stale_heartbeat_stepdown and found the guard I had missed, so the suspicion is refuted rather than confirmed. appendEntries returns early on an older term ('Ignore an older term', inside raft.go 1458-1606) before the block that adopts the term and moves the node to Follower, so the transition condition I relied on is unreachable for older terms. The probe history is real — a genuine election with six committed entries, the leader's term and header recorded and a heartbeat payload built exactly as the heartbeat goroutine builds it (header, term, leader address, no previous entry, no leader commit index), a real partition, a real second leader at a higher term — and delivering that payload to the current leader left it Leader with term 3 and persisted term 3 unchanged, the response carrying its own term rather than the stale term, and the third voter's leader view unchanged. This update therefore records the negative result (conclusion explained with defect_claim refuted, the question's hypothesis revised under its own id, and the limits stated: AppendEntries only, one cluster size, one delivery time, equal-term delivery to a non-follower still performs the transition by design), and requests the retained receipt for the three assertions that hold when the guard works. That also closes the concurrency reading of the heartbeat fast path: for this message shape the handler touches only guarded fields and returns immediately, reconciling the api.go concurrent-safety claim with the handler's main-thread-only comment instead of leaving them contradictory.\"}"}}
